(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 19-year-old man develops an intensely pruritic vesicular eruption in linear streaks two days after clearing brush. What immunological mechanism underlies allergic contact dermatitis, and what is its most common cause in the United States?","choices":["An immune complex mediated type III hypersensitivity reaction to a topical allergen","A cell-mediated, delayed type IV hypersensitivity reaction, most commonly to urushiol sap","An immunoglobulin E mediated, immediate type I hypersensitivity reaction, most commonly to urushiol sap","Direct chemical disruption of the skin barrier without immune involvement","A cell-mediated, delayed type IV hypersensitivity reaction, most commonly to nickel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Allergic contact dermatitis is an immunologic cell-mediated, delayed, type IV hypersensitivity reaction. Urushiol sap of Toxicodendron species, found in poison ivy, poison sumac, and poison oak, is the most common cause, and roughly 50 to 75 percent of people in the United States are allergic to these plants. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman with dermatitis herpetiformis achieves rapid relief on dapsone and asks whether that is the whole treatment. What is the most important additional element of management?","choices":["Lifelong systemic corticosteroids","A strict gluten-free diet for six weeks, after which gluten may be reintroduced","A strict lifelong gluten-free diet, since the condition is intrinsically linked to gluten sensitivity","High-potency topical steroids as the sole long-term therapy","Indefinite dapsone monotherapy alone, since dietary change does not affect the cutaneous disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dapsone provides rapid symptom relief but the long-term management is a strict gluten-free diet, because the disease is driven by immunoglobulin A antibodies against epidermal transglutaminase in patients consuming dietary gluten. All patients should also be screened for celiac disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 6-year-old has fifteen discrete, smooth, firm, flesh-coloured dome-shaped papules averaging 4 mm on the trunk and arms, each with a central dimple. His parents ask about treatment. What is a reasonable approach?","choices":["Aggressive curettage of every lesion at the first visit, whatever the number of lesions or the child's age","Oral acyclovir","Observation is appropriate for many patients, since procedures may blister, pigment, or scar","Topical mupirocin to each lesion","Salicylic acid 40 percent to each lesion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Molluscum contagiosum is a benign poxvirus infection, and observation is appropriate for many patients because procedures may blister, pigment, or scar. Approved options such as berdazimer gel and cantharidin exist when treatment is chosen. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 136"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with cellulitis of the leg has an area that is tense, cyanotic, and bronzed with blanching. What is the significance of this finding?","choices":["The antibiotic dose should be increased and the area observed","This indicates a drug reaction to the antibiotic","The tissue is devitalised and will not be perfused, so antibiotics cannot reach it and surgical debridement is needed","This indicates a deep vein thrombosis and therapeutic anticoagulation should be started immediately without imaging","This is the expected appearance of resolving cellulitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Devitalised tissue that is tense, cyanotic, necrotic, bronzed, and blanched will not be perfused, so antibiotics will not get to the site. Surgical debridement is required. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A 14-year-old boy with neurofibromatosis type 1 has a large, diffuse soft tissue mass running along a nerve in the thigh. What is a plexiform neurofibroma, and what is the concern with it?","choices":["A discrete benign nerve sheath tumour protruding just above the skin surface, sessile or pedunculated","A pigmented macule that grows in proportion to the child","A cluster of freckles smaller than 5 mm in intertriginous areas","A tumour in the tissue covering nerves that is large and extensive and may be locally invasive","A tumour arising exclusively within the brain and spinal cord"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Plexiform neurofibromas are tumours in the tissue covering nerves, occurring anywhere except the brain and spinal cord. They are large and extensive and may be locally invasive, so clinical evaluation is needed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with no symptoms attends for a routine health check, while a 55-year-old woman attends the same clinic with a specific complaint requiring evaluation. How does screening testing differ in purpose from diagnostic testing?","choices":["Screening confirms a diagnosis already suspected from the history and examination, whereas diagnostic testing looks for occult disease in people without symptoms","Diagnostic testing replaces the history and physical examination","Both have identical purposes and are interchangeable","Screening identifies the likelihood of occult disease, whereas diagnostic testing complements history and examination to reduce uncertainty and guide management","Screening is used only in emergency medicine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Screening testing identifies the likelihood of occult disease. Diagnostic testing complements the history and physical examination, reduces uncertainty about diagnosis or prognosis, and helps decide management. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 10-year-old girl with red hair has multiple freckles across the nose and cheeks, and her mother asks what can be done about them. What is the cornerstone of management for ephelides?","choices":["No management is possible or appropriate for these pigmented lesions","Systemic depigmenting therapy","Sun protection, with patient education and counselling as the key element","Surgical excision of each macule","Cryotherapy applied to each lesion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sun protection is the cornerstone of management for ephelides, with proper patient education and counselling described as key. Topical depigmenting agents such as hydroquinone, retinoids, alpha-hydroxy acids, and botanicals may be used, and intense pulsed light is an option. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has had erythematous scaly patches and plaques on the trunk for four years, several larger than 5 cm, that have been treated as psoriasis and eczema without lasting response. Pruritus is prominent. What features discriminate this from the inflammatory conditions it resembles?","choices":["The presence of scale","Chronicity, treatment resistance, and large or oddly distributed lesions","Involvement of the trunk","The presence of pruritus","A positive potassium hydroxide preparation taken from an advancing scaly edge"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mycosis fungoides may resemble psoriasis, eczema, or tinea, and the discriminators are chronicity, treatment resistance, and large or oddly distributed lesions. Early morphology is localised or generalised erythematous patches or scaly plaques, usually on the trunk, frequently greater than 5 cm. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man returns from a hotel stay with several itchy welts on exposed skin. Examination shows wheals and papules with a haemorrhagic punctum, arranged in rows of three. He did not feel the bites occur. What organism is responsible, and what is the classic descriptive term?","choices":["Cimex lectularius, with rows of three bites known as breakfast, lunch, and dinner","Cercariae, producing urticaria-like lesions after water exposure","Pediculus humanus corporis, producing linear excoriations on the back and waist","Sarcoptes scabiei, with the burrow known as the delta-wing jet","Tunga penetrans, producing translucent nodules on the feet"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bedbugs are nocturnal feeders that hide in cracks and crevices of headboards, picture frames, and behind loose wallpaper. Bites are painless, multiple, and grouped in a linear fashion, and a row of three bites is known as breakfast, lunch, and dinner. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with alcohol use disorder develops severe leg pain and rapidly progressive skin changes after a minor injury. Which set of risk factors is associated with necrotizing fasciitis?","choices":["Obesity, poor hygiene, occlusive clothing, and hot humid temperatures","Continuous immersion of the hands in water and contact with chemicals","Manicure, ingrown nail, hangnail, and nail biting","Trauma, burns, surgery, immunosuppression, renal failure, alcoholism, odontogenic infection, and intravenous drug use, with male predominance","Impaired lymphatic drainage after mastectomy, athlete's foot as a portal of entry, obesity, trauma, and a pre-existing skin infection such as impetigo"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Those are the listed risk factors, and the condition is more common in males than females. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man who has worked outdoors for forty years is examined. Several pink papules 0.3 to 0.5 cm are visible on the scalp, and running a hand across the skin reveals further lesions that could not be seen at all. What is the characteristic tactile finding of an actinic keratosis, and what does it imply for examination technique?","choices":["A pearly translucent quality with visible telangiectasias, most apparent when the skin is stretched","A stuck-on quality that lifts at the edge","A velvety texture, best appreciated on inspection alone","A boggy fluctuant quality on palpation","A sandpaper texture, meaning a lesion may be more apparent by touch than by sight"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Actinic keratoses are small 0.2 to 0.6 cm flesh-coloured, pink, or slightly hyperpigmented papules with a characteristic sandpaper texture on palpation, and a lesion may be more apparent by touch than by sight. The skin must therefore be palpated, not only inspected. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman is treated for scabies and asks what she should do with her sheets and clothing. What environmental measure is specified for bedding and clothing?","choices":["Wash them, or set them aside for 14 days in a plastic bag in a warm area, with high heat of 60 degrees Celsius needed to kill mites","No environmental measures are needed because transmission requires prolonged direct skin-to-skin contact for 15 to 20 minutes","Wash them in cold water, since detergent alone kills the mites","Set them aside for 24 hours, after which the mites die","Discard all bedding and clothing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bedding and clothing should be washed or set aside for 14 days in a plastic bag in a warm area, and high heat at 60 degrees Celsius is needed to kill mites. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman presents with chronic bilateral lower-leg swelling, pruritus, and skin discoloration. Examination demonstrates poorly demarcated erythematous plaques, pitting edema, and brown hyperpigmentation around both medial ankles. Pedal pulses are diminished. What is the most appropriate next step before prescribing compression therapy?","choices":["Begin oral cephalexin","Perform an ankle-brachial index","Order patch testing","Perform a skin biopsy","Obtain a bacterial wound culture"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Compression is the cornerstone of management only once adequate arterial circulation has been established. An ankle-brachial index or toe pressure should be obtained when peripheral arterial disease is suspected — as the diminished pulses here suggest — before initiating substantial compression. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 17-year-old boy starting topical tretinoin and benzoyl peroxide for acne asks how to use them. What education should be given?","choices":["Apply the two together to improve penetration and speed the response","Apply both twice daily and expect clearance within one week","Use only one of the two, since they cannot be used in the same regimen","Do not apply the two together; wait at least three hours between them because of the risk of skin irritation","Apply the two together but wash the face vigorously with hot water between the two applications each day"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Topical tretinoin and benzoyl peroxide should not be applied together because of the risk of skin irritation, and at least three hours should separate them. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has folliculitis that has recurred repeatedly despite topical treatment, and cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus each time. What additional measure addresses the likely reservoir?","choices":["Switching to an antibacterial soap alone","Oral ciprofloxacin","Mupirocin ointment applied to the nares to address the Staphylococcus aureus carrier state","A potassium hydroxide wet mount of a plucked hair","A longer course of the same topical antibiotic cream applied twice daily for a further six weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent or recalcitrant folliculitis raises the Staphylococcus aureus carrier state, and mupirocin ointment is used for it. Nasal swabs of the patient and family members are taken to evaluate carriage. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has a healing surgical wound and asks why the scar keeps getting stronger over months. What are the four phases of wound healing, and what accounts for the improving tensile strength of a maturing scar?","choices":["Hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, and remodeling, with tensile strength fixed at the time of wound closure and unchanged thereafter","Hemostasis, proliferation, contraction, and epithelialisation, with strength from fibroblast proliferation","Inflammation, proliferation, remodeling, and contracture, with strength from new elastin deposition","Inflammation, granulation, maturation, and involution, with strength from vascular ingrowth","Hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, and remodeling, with tensile strength improving through progressive cross-linking of collagen fibres"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The four phases are hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, and remodeling. As the scar matures its tensile strength improves because of progressive cross-linking of collagen fibres. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 3-month-old infant with blistering at sites of minor friction is being investigated to establish where in the skin the split is occurring. What diagnostic test is considered the gold standard for establishing the level of cleavage in epidermolysis bullosa?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide preparation of blister fluid","Direct immunofluorescence for immunoglobulin A deposits","Immunofluorescence antigen mapping","Skin biopsy with transmission electron microscopy","Genetic testing alone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Skin biopsy with transmission electron microscopy is the gold standard for determining the cleavage plane. Immunofluorescence antigen mapping localises missing or reduced proteins, and genetic testing is confirmatory and guides prognosis and family counselling. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with diabetes has three thickened, discoloured, crumbling toenails with distal onycholysis. He asks for oral terbinafine. What must be done first?","choices":["Remove the affected nails surgically before medical therapy","Start terbinafine empirically, since dermatophytes cause most cases","Obtain a Wood's lamp examination of the nails","Begin topical antifungal therapy, which is adequate for most nail infections without oral therapy","Confirm fungal infection before oral therapy, because many dystrophic nails are not fungal"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Fungus must be confirmed before oral therapy, since many dystrophic nails are not fungal. Testing options include potassium hydroxide microscopy, periodic acid-Schiff stain of nail clippings, fungal culture, or polymerase chain reaction, sampling the most proximal accessible diseased nail bed. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 19-year-old presents in summer with numerous well-defined hypopigmented macules over the upper back and shoulders that do not tan. Scraping the surface reveals fine scale. What does potassium hydroxide microscopy show?","choices":["Long septate branching hyphae without yeast forms","Budding yeast with pseudohyphae only","No organisms at all, since the diagnosis is made only by skin biopsy","Short hyphae with clusters of yeast, described as spaghetti and meatballs","Mites, eggs, and fecal pellets"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Potassium hydroxide microscopy in pityriasis versicolor shows short hyphae with clusters of yeast, classically described as spaghetti and meatballs. The condition is caused by overgrowth of lipid-dependent Malassezia species that normally inhabit the skin. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman presents with right-sided facial weakness, painful vesicles in the right ear canal, and vertigo. What is the diagnosis, and what treatment is added beyond antivirals?","choices":["Ramsay Hunt syndrome, treated with an antiviral plus a systemic corticosteroid started early when not contraindicated","Ramsay Hunt syndrome, treated with an antiviral alone","Otitis externa, treated with topical antibiotic drops","Bell palsy, treated with a corticosteroid alone","Herpes zoster ophthalmicus, treated with a systemic antiviral and urgent ophthalmology referral for corneal assessment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ramsay Hunt syndrome, or herpes zoster oticus, is peripheral facial palsy with painful vesicles of the ear canal, auricle, or oropharynx, sometimes with hearing loss, tinnitus, or vertigo. Antiviral therapy plus a systemic corticosteroid is started early when not contraindicated. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 5-month-old girl has a large hemangioma of the face lying close to the eye and the corner of the mouth. What complications should be considered?","choices":["Malignant transformation to angiosarcoma","Compression of vital structures, blocked vision, interference with feeding or respiration, obstruction of the external auditory canal, and extracutaneous lesions","Progressive darkening and thickening of the lesion throughout life, with growth in proportion to the child and no involution at any stage of childhood or adolescence","No complications occur, since all lesions involute","Systemic hypertension from arteriovenous shunting"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hemangiomas may compress vital structures, block vision, interfere with feeding or respiration, obstruct the external auditory canal, and occur at extracutaneous sites. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl develops an abrupt eruption of numerous small raindrop-shaped scaly papules and plaques over the trunk and proximal limbs. Her mother reports she was treated for a sore throat about two and a half weeks ago. She is otherwise well. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Oral methotrexate","Topical mupirocin to the affected areas","Oral acitretin","Reassurance, with phototherapy or topical steroids only if needed","A tapering course of systemic corticosteroids given over four weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Guttate psoriasis presents as raindrop-shaped erythematous papules and plaques, typically in children 2 to 3 weeks following a streptococcal infection or upper respiratory infection. No treatment is needed, though phototherapy and topical steroids may be used. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 164"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man develops severe nocturnal itching six weeks after his partner was treated for scabies. How is scabies typically transmitted, and how long after infestation does pruritus appear?","choices":["Brief casual contact, with pruritus appearing within 24 hours","Contact with contaminated water, with pruritus and a prickling sensation appearing within hours of exposure","Close physical contact for 15 to 20 minutes, with pruritus appearing 4 to 6 weeks after initial infestation","Direct head-to-head contact, with symptoms after 4 to 6 weeks","Airborne spread, with pruritus appearing after 1 week"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Scabies is transmitted by close physical contact for 15 to 20 minutes and may also be acquired from the bedding or underclothing of an infested individual. Pruritus appears 4 to 6 weeks after initial infestation, and many patients do not develop symptoms for up to 3 months. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with tender pretibial nodules undergoes a deep incisional biopsy. What finding confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Full-thickness epidermal necrosis","Septal panniculitis without vasculitis, with Miescher's granulomas","Palisading granulomas with central necrobiosis and mucin","A dense dermal neutrophilic infiltrate with no organisms","Lobular panniculitis with vasculitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy shows septal panniculitis without vasculitis and Miescher's granulomas. A deep incisional biopsy is needed because the pathology lies in the subcutaneous fat. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man is referred with a red, warm, tender left lower leg. He has bilateral chronic ankle swelling and brown discoloration of both gaiter regions, but the left leg has become acutely more swollen and painful over two days. He is afebrile. What consideration should most urgently shape the evaluation?","choices":["Compression therapy should be started immediately to relieve the swelling","Bilateral involvement makes cellulitis the most likely explanation","The brown discoloration indicates active infection requiring culture","Absence of fever excludes an acute vascular event","Acute unilateral swelling or pain should raise concern for deep venous thrombosis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The differential for stasis dermatitis explicitly flags that acute unilateral swelling or pain should raise concern for deep venous thrombosis. Chronic bilateral changes with a new unilateral acute change is precisely that pattern. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy is brought in with fever and a rash. Examination shows non-blanching deposits of blood in the skin, several measuring 6 mm across. What is the most appropriate interpretation of this finding?","choices":["Ecchymosis from minor trauma, requiring no further action","A wheal produced by fluid infiltration into the dermis","Purpura, which should be treated as a medical emergency until proven otherwise","Petechiae, which should be treated as a medical emergency until proven otherwise","Purpura, which is a benign finding that can be observed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Purpura is a deposit of blood 4 mm or greater, and the lecture flags it explicitly as a medical emergency until proven otherwise. Combined with fever in a child this demands immediate evaluation. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 9-month-old boy is brought in for a diaper rash of one week. Examination shows beefy erythema involving the inguinal folds, with peripheral scale and several small papules and pustules on the thighs a short distance beyond the main area of redness. He completed a course of amoxicillin two weeks ago. What finding most strongly indicates the infectious complication rather than simple irritant disease?","choices":["The presence of erythema in the diaper area","The one-week duration of the eruption","Involvement of the inguinal folds with satellite lesions beyond the main area","The presence of peripheral scaling at the outer edge of the area of erythema","The recent course of amoxicillin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Whether the skin folds are involved is the finding that distinguishes irritant from candidal disease. Irritant diaper dermatitis affects the convex surfaces and generally spares the inguinal folds because they are less exposed to urine and feces; candidal disease produces beefy erythema in the folds with satellite papules and pustules. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man presents with a painful swelling in the natal cleft, and the clinician reviews what predisposed him to it. Which set of risk factors is associated with pilonidal disease?","choices":["Fair skin, sun exposure, and age over 40","Obesity, local trauma or irritation, sedentary lifestyle, increased hair density in the natal cleft, and family history, with a male to female ratio of about 3 to 1","Chronic venous insufficiency, impaired lymphatic drainage after mastectomy, obesity, and a previous episode of cellulitis, with a female predominance of about 3 to 1","Prematurity, low birth weight, and multiple gestation","Female sex, pregnancy, and oestrogen excess"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Those are the listed risk factors, and the male to female ratio is 3 to 1. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man with plaque psoriasis reports painful, stiff finger joints. He describes more than thirty minutes of morning stiffness that improves as he uses his hands during the day. Examination shows diffuse swelling of the entire second and third digits of the right hand. What additional finding is most characteristic of the associated condition?","choices":["A positive rheumatoid factor in most patients","Association with human leukocyte antigen Cw6","Stiffness that worsens with activity through the day","Symmetric involvement of the small joints with sparing of the distal joints","Association with human leukocyte antigen B27"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Psoriatic arthritis is associated with human leukocyte antigen B27. The presentation described — psoriasis with joint pains frequently of the hands, stiffness lasting more than thirty minutes that is relieved with activity, and sausage digits — is the classic picture. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 161"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man has maceration and erythematous erosions between the third and fourth toes with associated fissuring. What form of tinea pedis is this, and is it the commonest?","choices":["Interdigital tinea pedis, which is the most common form","Interdigital tinea pedis, which is the least common form","Hyperkeratotic tinea pedis, which is the most common form","Vesiculobullous tinea pedis, which is the most common form","Candidal intertrigo of the toe webs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Interdigital tinea pedis is the most common form, caused by Trichophyton species, producing maceration and erythematous erosions or scales between the toes, especially in the third and fourth interspaces, with associated fissures. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 36-year-old pregnant woman and her 7-year-old son both develop erythema migrans after a camping trip. What is first-line oral antibiotic therapy for Lyme disease, and what alternative applies to children and pregnant women with early disease?","choices":["Permethrin applied topically to the bite site","Doxycycline first line, with amoxicillin as an alternative first-line agent for early disease in children and pregnant women","Amoxicillin first line for all patients including adults, with doxycycline reserved for treatment failure or late disease","No antibiotics for stage 1, since erythema migrans is self-limited","Intravenous ceftriaxone for all stages"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics are indicated in all stages. Doxycycline is first line, with amoxicillin as an alternative first-line agent for early disease including erythema migrans, particularly in children and pregnant women. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with erythema multiforme triggered by recurrent herpes simplex virus has had four episodes in twelve months. What management addresses the recurrences?","choices":["Suppressive oral acyclovir or valacyclovir","Topical corticosteroids applied continuously to previously affected sites","Oral antihistamines taken continuously between episodes","A short course of oral prednisone at the first sign of each episode","Avoidance of non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and sulfonamides"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oral acyclovir or valacyclovir is used when the eruption is triggered by herpes simplex virus, and a prior episode is itself a risk factor for recurrence. Suppressing the trigger is what prevents the next episode. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has a hard, conical keratotic projection on the dorsum of his hand. What is the key principle in evaluating this lesion?","choices":["A conical shape reliably indicates a benign process","The horn itself should be sent for histology and the lesion at its base left undisturbed and unsampled for the time being","Cryotherapy should be applied without histological assessment","The lesion can be reassured about if it is painless","There is often no clinical feature distinguishing benign from malignant, so the underlying lesion at the base must be diagnosed"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A cutaneous horn arises from the surface of another lesion, which may be benign or malignant — actinic keratosis, warts, and squamous cell carcinoma among them. There is often no clinical feature to distinguish them, so the underlying lesion must be diagnosed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has been applying a topical corticosteroid to the same area of eyelid skin for several months. What adverse effects should be anticipated?","choices":["Permanent loss of hair follicles at the treated site","Atrophy, striae, telangiectasia, and hypopigmentation","Nephrotoxicity with prolonged application","Photosensitivity, dryness, and irritation","Hyperpigmentation, hypertrichosis, and thickening of the dermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prolonged use of topical corticosteroids may cause atrophy, striae, telangiectasia, and hypopigmentation. This is why potency is selected according to site and severity and why the usual course is twice daily for two weeks. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy has a circumscribed light brown patch on the trunk resembling a café au lait spot, within which several darker pigmented macules and papules are visible. What is this lesion and how is it managed?","choices":["A dysplastic naevus requiring biopsy","Nevus spilus, requiring immediate complete surgical excision because of a high risk of progression to melanoma during childhood","A café au lait macule of neurofibromatosis type 1","A blue nevus arising in adolescence","Nevus spilus, a variant of congenital naevus, managed with observation, periodic clinical evaluation, and sun protection counselling"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Nevus spilus, the spotted nevus, is a variant of congenital naevus present at birth or in the first years of life, most commonly on the trunk and extremities. Darkly pigmented macules or papules sit within a background that is circumscribed and similar in appearance to a café au lait spot, and it rarely progresses to melanoma. Management is observation, periodic evaluation, and sun protection. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man in equatorial Africa presents with rapidly progressive violaceous nodules on both legs. He is not known to have human immunodeficiency virus infection and is not taking immunosuppressive therapy. Which clinical form of Kaposi sarcoma is described as often aggressive and potentially rapidly fatal in young Black men in equatorial Africa?","choices":["The classic form","The endemic form","The epidemic form","The iatrogenic form","The nodular form"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The endemic form is often aggressive disease in young Black men in equatorial Africa and can be rapidly fatal, in contrast to the classic form in older men, which follows a chronic course and is rarely fatal. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl is evaluated for a lesion on the finger pad that appeared rapidly after she injured the finger and bleeds easily when bumped. Examination shows a bright red, friable, pedunculated papule. What is the diagnosis, and what is notable about its name?","choices":["Pyogenic granuloma, which is misnamed because it is neither infectious nor granulomatous","Pyogenic granuloma, so named because it is a granulomatous response to bacterial infection","Dermatofibroma","Cherry angioma","Amelanotic melanoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pyogenic granuloma is an acquired vascular lesion that is misnamed, being neither infectious nor granulomatous. It is a benign vascular tumour common in children, young adults, and pregnancy, arising as a response to injury or hormonal factors, most often on the head, neck, and fingers. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 29-year-old hairdresser reports recurrent episodes of intensely itchy blisters on her hands. Examination shows multiple grouped 1 to 2 mm vesicles on the lateral aspects of the fingers and the adjacent interdigital surfaces, some coalescing, on a poorly defined pink base with surrounding dryness. She reports previous episodes that persisted for several weeks, dried up, and then peeled. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Irritant contact dermatitis","Dyshidrotic eczema","Atopic dermatitis of the hands","Nummular eczema","Allergic contact dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dyshidrotic eczema is a chronic, relapsing eruption of intensely pruritic vesicles on the hands and feet, classically described as tapioca vesicles. They may coalesce into bullae, persist for several weeks, then desiccate and resolve with desquamation — the exact course she describes. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman has a tender fluctuant abscess of the buttock that has not drained spontaneously. What is the appropriate management?","choices":["Observation until the lesion drains on its own","Oral antibiotics selected by culture of the intact skin surface","Squeezing the lesion to express its contents","Warm soaks and broad-spectrum antibiotics alone","Surgical incision and drainage"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. If the lesion does not spontaneously drain, surgical incision and drainage is required. If it drains spontaneously, warm soaks and broad-spectrum antibiotics with consideration of methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus are used, adjusted by culture. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"A 27-year-old pregnant woman has several lesions on the upper trunk consisting of a central arteriole with radiating capillaries. What is the mechanism, and what should be asked in the history?","choices":["Proliferation of capillaries occurring with increasing age; no specific history of pregnancy, hormone use, alcohol, or hepatotoxic medication is required","Dilation of pre-existing vessels without proliferation, possibly from oestrogen excess; ask about pregnancy, hormone use, alcohol, and hepatotoxic medications","A benign vascular tumour arising after minor trauma","Extravasation of blood into the dermis","Dilated superficial dermal capillaries present from birth"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nevus araneus involves no vascular proliferation and is due to dilation of pre-existing vessels. Oestrogen excess states may be the cause, and lesions resolve after delivery or cessation of hormonal contraception. History should cover pregnancies, hormone use, alcohol, and high-risk medications for liver damage. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman reports gradual hair thinning over several years. Examination shows diffuse thinning across the central and parietal scalp with preservation of the frontal hairline. There are no bald patches, no scaling, no scarring, and no exclamation point hairs. Several of her male relatives began balding in their twenties. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Female-pattern hair loss","Lichen planopilaris","Tinea capitis","Male-pattern hair loss","Alopecia areata"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Female-pattern hair loss produces diffuse thinning of the central and parietal scalp without significant change to the frontal hairline. There is an increased frequency of balding among first-degree male relatives, which the family history here supplies. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 146"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy develops multiple erythematous papules arranged in linear streaks after handling a caterpillar. What is the term for the aggregate of medical effects caused by caterpillars, moths, and butterflies, and what is a specific step in management?","choices":["Tungiasis, treated with excision","Erucism, treated only with high-potency systemic corticosteroids and analgesia","Arachnidism, treated with antivenom","Cercarial dermatitis, treated with oatmeal baths","Lepidopterism, with removal of the hairs by stripping the affected area"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Lepidopterism is the aggregate of medical effects caused by caterpillars, moths, and butterflies. Gypsy moth caterpillar contact causes erucism, a pruritic dermatitis with erythematous papules in linear streaks. Management is symptomatic, and the hairs are removed by stripping. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy has several lesions around the nose and mouth. Examination shows golden-yellow adherent crusts over shallow erosions, with palpable regional lymphadenopathy. He is afebrile and there are only four lesions. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Topical mupirocin ointment, with crusts removed before application","Oral penicillin V","Oral cephalexin","Oral trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for presumed methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus","Topical mupirocin ointment applied over the intact crusts"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical therapy is used for a single or limited number of non-bullous lesions, and mupirocin ointment is adequate for most cases of impetigo and as effective as oral therapy with fewer side effects. Crusts should be removed before applying it. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with obesity, type 2 diabetes, and polycystic ovary syndrome has velvety hyperpigmented plaques of the neck and axillae. What is the primary management?","choices":["Topical retinoids as the primary treatment","Reassurance with no intervention, since the condition is purely cosmetic","Topical corticosteroids twice daily","Treat the underlying cause with weight loss and glycaemic control","Laser therapy or dermabrasion as the primary treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Primary management is treating the underlying cause through weight loss and glycaemic control, discontinuing any offending drug. Metformin reduces insulin resistance and may improve the skin findings. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man develops an exaggerated sunburn-like reaction on sun-exposed skin within hours of starting a new medication. The reaction is dose-dependent and occurred on the very first exposure. What mechanism is responsible, and what test is the gold standard for the alternative mechanism?","choices":["Phototoxicity, which is non-immunologic and dose-dependent; photopatch testing is the gold standard for photoallergy","Phytophotodermatitis from furanocoumarin contact; a thorough drug history is the gold standard","Phototoxicity, which is immunologic and dose-independent; minimal erythema dose testing is the gold standard for photoallergy","Polymorphous light eruption, which appears 30 minutes to hours after exposure; antinuclear antibody testing is the gold standard","Photoallergy, which is immunologic and requires prior sensitisation; photopatch testing is the gold standard for phototoxicity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Phototoxicity is non-immunologic and dose-dependent — the drug absorbs ultraviolet energy, generating reactive oxygen species and direct cellular damage that resembles an exaggerated sunburn. Photopatch testing is the gold standard for photoallergy, the other mechanism. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy has culture-confirmed tinea capitis and the species result will determine which drug he receives. Which oral agent is generally favoured for Trichophyton infection, and which for Microsporum?","choices":["Griseofulvin for Trichophyton and terbinafine for Microsporum","Terbinafine for Trichophyton and griseofulvin for Microsporum","Terbinafine for both organisms","Fluconazole for both organisms","Topical ketoconazole for both organisms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Terbinafine is generally favoured for Trichophyton infection and griseofulvin is often favoured for Microsporum infection, which is why identifying the organism by culture matters before prolonged systemic therapy. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 3-year-old has several tense, clear-to-cloudy blisters on the trunk arising on intact skin. Some have ruptured, leaving shallow moist erosions ringed by a collarette of scale. There is no lymphadenopathy. What organism and mechanism are responsible?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pyogenes, invading through a break in the skin","An autoimmune antibody against the epithelial basement membrane","Staphylococcus aureus exclusively, releasing epidermolytic toxins that cause epidermal splitting","Streptococcus pyogenes exclusively, through direct dermal invasion","Corynebacterium minutissimum invading the stratum corneum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bullous impetigo is caused exclusively by Staphylococcus aureus, which releases epidermolytic toxins producing epidermal splitting. Bullae occur on intact skin, rupture to leave collarettes, and lymphadenopathy is uncommon — in contrast to non-bullous disease, where it is common. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man living in a shelter has linear excoriations of the back and waist, while a 6-year-old girl in the same clinic has an itchy scalp. How does the presentation of pediculosis corporis differ from that of pediculosis capitis?","choices":["Pediculosis corporis produces burrows in the interdigital webs","Pediculosis corporis is transmitted by direct head-to-head contact","Pediculosis corporis affects the scalp with pruritus, low-grade fever, regional lymphadenopathy, and irritability rather than the trunk in children","Pediculosis corporis produces linear excoriations primarily on the back, neck, shoulders, and waist, with post-inflammatory pigmentation in chronic cases","Pediculosis corporis is always asymptomatic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Body lice produce pruritus with linear excoriations primarily on the back, neck, shoulders, and waist, and post-inflammatory pigmentation in chronic cases. Head lice affect the scalp with pruritus, low-grade fever, regional lymphadenopathy, and irritability. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman asks about treatment for photoaging. What topical agent is approved for this indication, and what should she be told to expect?","choices":["Calcipotriene, a vitamin D analogue","Tretinoin, with visible results expected within two weeks and no initial irritation at all","Tretinoin, with initial retinoid dermatitis expected and a minimum of 6 to 12 months of use","Topical corticosteroids applied nightly","Hydroquinone, which reverses the structural changes of photoaging"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tretinoin 0.025 to 0.1 percent is the only approved topical agent for photoaging. It stimulates collagen synthesis, inhibits matrix metalloproteinase activity, and reduces fine lines and dyspigmentation; therapy is started low and titrated, with initial retinoid dermatitis expected. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 22-year-old bartender develops streaky, bizarrely shaped erythema and blistering on the forearms two days after an outdoor shift squeezing limes. The affected areas later become hyperpigmented. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Phytophotodermatitis","Polymorphous light eruption","Allergic contact dermatitis to citrus","Drug-induced phototoxicity","Ordinary sunburn"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Phytophotodermatitis follows contact with furanocoumarins, also called psoralens, found in limes, celery, parsley, and related plants, followed by ultraviolet exposure. The streaky shapes trace where the juice ran, and postinflammatory hyperpigmentation follows. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A student proposes a rare diagnosis to explain a very typical presentation in a 30-year-old woman. What does the principle 'place your bets on uncommon manifestations of common conditions rather than common manifestations of uncommon conditions' mean in practice?","choices":["A typical presentation of a rare disease should be favoured over an atypical presentation of a common one","An atypical presentation of a frequent disease is more likely than a typical presentation of a rare one","Rare diagnoses should never be considered","The most serious diagnosis should always be assumed first","All diagnoses on a differential are equally likely until tested"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Common things occur commonly — when you hear hoof beats think of horses, not zebras. An unusual presentation of a common condition is more probable than a textbook presentation of something rare. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man has multiple actinic keratoses scattered across a bald scalp, and the clinician explains that treating only the individual visible lesions may be insufficient. What concept does this reflect?","choices":["Koebner phenomenon, in which lesions arise at sites of trauma","Paraneoplastic change, in which an internal malignancy drives the skin findings","Photoallergy, in which sensitisation spreads to unexposed skin","Field cancerisation, in which the surrounding sun-damaged skin also carries mutations","Pathergy, in which trauma to the skin provokes new lesions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Actinic keratoses represent a field cancerisation process, meaning the ultraviolet-induced mutations extend through the surrounding skin rather than being confined to the visible lesions. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man still has burning, stabbing pain four months after a thoracic zoster eruption cleared. How is postherpetic neuralgia defined, and what are the first-line treatments?","choices":["Pain persisting at least 90 days after rash onset, treated first with long-term opioids","Pain persisting at least 7 days after rash onset, treated with antivirals","Pain persisting at least 30 days after rash onset, treated first with systemic corticosteroids and a tapering course of oral prednisone over several weeks","Pain persisting at least 90 days after rash onset, treated first with gabapentin or pregabalin, an appropriate tricyclic antidepressant, or topical lidocaine","Any pain during the acute eruptive phase, treated with acetaminophen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pain persisting 90 days or more after rash onset is the commonly used definition. First-line treatment is gabapentin or pregabalin, an appropriate tricyclic antidepressant, or topical lidocaine, with a capsaicin patch as a further option. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman has a classic presentation of erysipelas with a sharply demarcated raised plaque of the shin and a fever of 39 degrees Celsius. What laboratory and imaging approach is appropriate?","choices":["Clinical diagnosis, with blood and tissue cultures not cost effective and imaging of low yield","Skin biopsy in every patient to confirm the diagnosis","Blood and tissue cultures in every patient before antibiotics are started, whatever the presentation","Magnetic resonance imaging to define the depth of involvement","No laboratory testing of any kind is ever useful"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Erysipelas is a clinical diagnosis in a classic presentation. Leukocytosis and raised erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are common, but blood and tissue cultures are not cost effective because of extremely low organism yield, and imaging studies are of low yield and not indicated. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 15-year-old presents with molluscum lesions confined to the genital area. What is the appropriate approach?","choices":["Assess for sexually transmitted infections as appropriate, since genital lesions in adolescents and adults may be sexually transmitted","Assume sexual abuse on the basis of the genital location alone","Refer immediately for surgical excision of all lesions","Reassure that genital molluscum is never sexually transmitted","Treat with oral acyclovir and screen for herpes simplex virus only, since no other infection needs to be considered in an adolescent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Genital lesions in adolescents and adults may be sexually transmitted and assessment for other infections is appropriate. In a child, genital lesions require context-sensitive assessment, and location alone does not prove abuse. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"A 6-week-old infant has a bright red papule on the shoulder, and the clinician classifies the lesion by the depth of vessel involvement. What are the three types of infantile hemangioma, and which is the most common?","choices":["Superficial, which is the most common, presenting as a bright red papule, plaque, or nodule, plus mixed and deep types","Capillary, cavernous, and arteriovenous, with capillary the most common","Proliferative, involuting, and involuted, with the proliferative type being the most common of the three at presentation","Congenital, infantile, and acquired, with congenital the most common","Deep, which is the most common, presenting as a pale or blue nodule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The superficial type is the most common, with dilated vessels in the dermis surrounded by proliferating endothelial cells, presenting as a bright red papule, plaque, or nodule and previously called a strawberry hemangioma. The deep type is the least common. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has a new pigmented longitudinal band in the nail of one thumb that has widened over six months and is broader proximally than distally. Pigment extends onto the proximal nail fold. What is the concern, and what is the sign at the nail fold called?","choices":["Nail unit melanoma, with pigment extending onto the periungual skin known as Hutchinson sign","Subungual haematoma, which migrates distally with nail growth","Benign longitudinal melanonychia, which typically affects multiple digits and does not widen proximally","Onychomycosis, causing discoloration of the nail plate","Glomus tumour, causing pain and cold sensitivity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. New or evolving longitudinal melanonychia in one digit with increasing width, irregular colour or spacing, and proximal widening or a triangular shape raises nail unit melanoma. Pigment on the periungual skin is Hutchinson sign, and the thumb and great toe are high-yield sites. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman presents with an intensely itchy eruption on both wrists and ankles that has been present for two months. Examination shows small, flat-topped, shiny violaceous papules, some coalescing into plaques, over the volar wrists and ankles. Fine white lines are visible across the surface of the plaques. She also has lacy white streaks on the buccal mucosa. What are the surface lines called?","choices":["Civatte bodies","Koebner phenomenon","Wickham striae","Collarette of scale","Auspitz sign"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fine white lines visible on the surface of lichen planus plaques are known as Wickham striae. Oral lichen planus can occur with or without cutaneous disease and often presents with erosive lesions or Wickham striae, as it does here. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 176"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has a chronic plaque on the ankle showing thickening and hardening of the skin with exaggerated surface markings, and adjacent skin showing accumulation of loose grey-white cornified fragments. What are these two secondary changes called?","choices":["Erosion and ulceration, respectively","Scaling and lichenification, respectively","Lichenification and scaling, respectively","Scar and fissure, respectively","Excoriation and crust, respectively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lichenification is thickening and hardening of the skin, and scaling is accumulation of loose or adherent cornified fragments of the epidermis, typically grey or white. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman presents on day four of a dermatomal vesicular eruption with new lesions still appearing. Within what window should antiviral therapy ideally be started, and when should it still be given beyond that window?","choices":["Only if postherpetic neuralgia has already developed","Within 7 days of rash onset, with no exceptions","Ideally within 72 hours of rash onset, and still given after 72 hours when new lesions are forming or the ophthalmic division is involved","Ideally within 72 hours of rash onset, with no benefit and no indication for antiviral therapy at any point beyond that window at all","Only after polymerase chain reaction confirmation, whenever that returns"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antivirals should be started as soon as possible, ideally within 72 hours of rash onset, and treatment is still given after 72 hours when new lesions are forming or there is ophthalmic involvement. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman is being counselled after a melanoma diagnosis and asks what most determines her outlook. What is the dominant prognostic variable in melanoma, and what must be ensured about it?","choices":["The presence of asymmetry, graded at initial assessment","Lesion diameter, which must exceed 6 mm at the time of the initial biopsy to be significant","Breslow thickness, which must be accurately measured at the time of the initial biopsy","The anatomic subtype of the melanoma","The patient's Fitzpatrick skin type"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Survival drops sharply with increasing Breslow thickness and with nodal or distant spread. Breslow thickness is the dominant prognostic variable and must be accurately measured at the time of initial biopsy — which is why a shave biopsy that transects the base can compromise staging. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A student is seeing a 46-year-old woman with an undifferentiated complaint and is unsure how to begin working through it. What are the first three steps of the clinical reasoning process?","choices":["Order diagnostic tests, interpret them, then take a history","Gather initial patient information, organise and interpret clinical information, then synthesise it into a problem representation","Generate hypotheses, gather information, then test the hypotheses","Treat the presenting signs and symptoms, observe the response to intervention, then arrive at the definitive diagnosis afterwards","Apply a clinical guideline, then confirm it fits the patient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The process begins with gathering initial patient information, then organising and interpreting it, then synthesising the information into a problem representation, before hypotheses are generated and tested. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has two round, sharply circumscribed, slightly erythematous scaly plaques on the forearm, each with an advancing scaly border and a clearer centre. Where should the specimen for potassium hydroxide microscopy be taken from?","choices":["A plucked hair from within the plaque","Normal skin adjacent to the lesion","The centre of the lesion, where the change began","The active advancing border of the lesion","A nail clipping from the same limb"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The potassium hydroxide specimen is taken from the active border, where the viable organism is concentrated. Culture is added for high clinical suspicion when the potassium hydroxide preparation is negative. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with a family history of melanoma has more than 100 naevi, several at least 5 mm with irregular, indistinct borders. How is a dysplastic naevus diagnosed, and what is the management?","choices":["Diagnosis is by biopsy, with observation, biopsy of all changing or developing lesions, excision where melanoma is a concern, and sun protection","Diagnosis is by dermoscopy alone, with no histology needed","Diagnosis is by biopsy, and all naevi should be excised prophylactically regardless of whether they are changing or developing over time at all","Diagnosis is clinical, and no biopsy is required at any point","No follow-up is needed once the lesions are documented"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diagnosis of a dysplastic melanocytic nevus is by biopsy. Management includes observation, biopsy of all changing or developing lesions, excision where there is concern for melanoma, and sun protection. Family history is a risk factor, and dysplastic nevus syndrome may produce over 100 naevi by adolescence. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl has moderate papulopustular and mixed acne of the face and upper back that has not responded to eight weeks of a topical antimicrobial. What regimen is recommended?","choices":["Oral isotretinoin as monotherapy","A topical retinoid together with an oral antibiotic, without benzoyl peroxide","An oral antibiotic alone continued indefinitely","A topical retinoid alone","A topical retinoid together with an oral antibiotic and topical benzoyl peroxide"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Moderate papulopustular and mixed acne is treated with a topical retinoid and an oral antibiotic and topical benzoyl peroxide. Benzoyl peroxide is specifically added whenever an antibiotic is used, to reduce the risk of bacterial resistance. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
